    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71214": {
      "title": "Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Information General Information Position Number 003744 Working Title Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Classification Title Associate Academic Dean College Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Department College of Arts + Architecture Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 12 Primary Purpose of Department The College of Arts + Architecture was formed in 2008 with the aim of combining the arts disciplines and architecture under one roof, bringing a critical mass of artists and designers together to elevate the College’s voice. As the only college of its kind in the UNC System, the COAA demonstrates UNC Charlotte’s commitment to arts and culture on campus and in the broader community, presenting exhibitions, lectures, and more than 100 performances each year. The College is home to three graduate degree programs, nine undergraduate programs, and certificate programs in Musical Theatre, Jazz, and Advanced Dance Studies, and a professional training certificate in dance in partnership with the Charlotte Ballet. The College is deeply committed to student retention and graduation and provides extensive resources, including academic and career advising, internships, and scholarships. Students and faculty all benefit from the College’s commitment to personalized advising from specialized, full-time professional academic advisors who work with students one-on-one throughout their course of study. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work Assist the Dean in the oversight of various duties, which may include but aren’t limited to, advancing research missions, supporting the undergraduate and graduate degree programs, faculty evaluation, faculty development, facilitate external support for undergraduate and graduate programs, curriculum development, course schedule planning, faculty/staff hiring and supervision, budget development, fiscal monitoring, and other related administrative activities with and for the assigned program areas. Reports directly to the Dean and may assume some duties of the Dean in their absence. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Essential Duties The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations works with the Chairs of the Dance, Music and Theatre Departments to schedule all internal and external performances (as well as curricular and programmatic events). The Associate Dean also supports instructional delivery in order to ensure the effective use of these spaces in a manner that advances departmental and College strategic goals and academic missions. This includes overseeing the use of performing arts spaces and venues in the COAA , operations and production budgets, and performing arts contracts and purchasing (in consultation with the Dean, Assistant Dean of Business and Personnel, and the Performing Arts Business Services Coordinator). The Associate Dean also works with the Performing Arts Operations Manager to manage the College’s performing arts venues, including Robinson Hall for the Performing Arts, the Johnson Band Center, and the Rowe Arts Building. The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations is a member of the College’s leadership team. As such, the Associate Dean collaborates with various COAA leaders, including the Director of Communications & External Relations, the Director of Galleries, the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, the Associate Dean for Research, the Director of Collaborative Works, and the Director of Assessment. Administrative duties include (but are not limited to) strategic and budget planning and management. The Associate Dean will also maintain a teaching load consisting of a minimum of three (3) credit hours and a maximum of six (6) credit hours per academic year. These courses may include advanced discipline-specific courses (production and/or performing arts), courses in the arts designed for non-majors (which constitute an important aspect of the university’s general education program), and courses offered as part of departmental and/or university honors programs. This position has eleven direct reports, including the Production Manager, two SHRA staff (Performing Arts Operations Manager and Audiences Services Director), two EHRA staff (Costume Lab Manager and Assistant Technical Director), and seven faculty members who complete the CoAA Design & Production Unit. This includes lecturers in Lighting Design, Costume Design, Stage Management, Sound Design, Technical Direction, and a Master Electrician. The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations oversees the academic mission of the Design & Production Unit (working in collaboration with the performing arts Chairs and the Production Manager). The Design & Production Unit supports college performances and events both directly and through practicum and introductory courses taught by unit lecturers. Description of the Work The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations will Assist the Dean in the oversight of various duties, which may include but aren’t limited to, advancing research missions, supporting the undergraduate and graduate degree programs, faculty and staff evaluation and development, facilitate external support for undergraduate and graduate programs, curriculum development, course schedule planning, performance and event planning, space scheduling and planning, faculty/staff hiring and supervision, budget development, fiscal monitoring, and other related administrative activities with and for the assigned program areas. Reports directly to the Dean and may assume some duties of the Dean in their absence. The Associate Dean also oversees the college’s performing arts spaces and venues with regard to their maintenance, equipment, and renovation. This includes communicating with Legal Affairs, Environmental Health and Safety, Police and Public Safety, and Risk Management and Insurance to ensure that performing arts operations and performances follow university and state policies regarding safety, minors on campus, licenses, contracts, and the use of campus facilities. The Associate Dean will also advise the Dean on other space-related matters in Robinson Hall, including academic, faculty/staff, and support spaces. Required Education, Skills, and Experience UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in one of the performing arts disciplines represented within the College (Dance, Music, or Theatre; this may also include Arts Education). Other disciplines that may be considered include Education or a related degree in the production arts. Demonstrated understanding of the relationship between design and production, performance planning, project management, project budgeting, and/or performing arts operational needs. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Experience with dance, music, and theatrical productions in an educational setting is preferred. Strong leadership skills and written and oral communication skills are preferred. The ability to manage multiple projects and teams concurrently is also desired. This role will collaboratively manage relationships across multiple disciplines with varying degrees of experience and knowledge; the ability to foster positive, collegial, and professional relationships is highly desired. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Job Open Date 09/16/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/01/2026 Proposed Hire Date 07/01/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other * Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents Abridged Portfolio Teaching Philosophy Online Portfolio Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71196": {
      "title": "Blumenthal Professor of Religious Studies",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Blumenthal Professor of Religious Studies Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Blumenthal Professor of Religious Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The Department of Religious Studies at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for the Blumenthal Professorship in Religious Studies, a full-time, tenured faculty position to begin 16 August 2027. The appointment will be made at the associate or full professor rank. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position #5853) and submit the following materials: 1) a letter of interest discussing the applicant’s professional qualifications and history regarding the five requirements and how the applicant would contribute to the department, college, and university’s research, teaching, and service mission; and 2) a curriculum vitae. Applicants selected for further consideration will be asked to send three confidential letters of recommendation to the search committee chair. Finalists will also be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage and include all UNC-Charlotte students while eliminating barriers to learning, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The candidate selected for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check and will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Sean McCloud, Chair, Department of Religious Studies ( spmcclou@charlotte.edu ). Position Information General Information Position Number 005853 Working Title Blumenthal Professor of Religious Studies Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department Religious Studies Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department Established in 1964, the Department of Religious Studies at UNC Charlotte has a long tradition of providing a multidisciplinary approach to the study of religions. We provide undergraduate and graduate instruction in the academic study of religion in a global context; conduct and publish research related to religion; and provide service that enhances public understanding of issues related to religion. The Department hosts the annual Loy H. Witherspoon Distinguished Lecture in Religious Studies, which is the oldest and most prestigious endowed lecture at UNC Charlotte. Religious Studies faculty contribute to the institutional work of the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences and the University. Our mission is to foster a critical understanding of the significance of religion in human societies and cultures. As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences ( CHESS ) houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 university, and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Blumenthal Professor of Religious Studies will serve as a catalyst for interdisciplinary teaching, research, and grant activity in the field of religious studies. The teacher-scholar hired for this position will be an advocate for the study of religion and the humanities, will be familiar with experiential and engaged pedagogies, and would be cross-listing their courses with other programs and departments. The successful candidate will be trained in the academic study of religion and experienced with a range of disciplinary methods and perspectives that inform teaching and research in the field. In addition to research and service the Blumenthal Professor will teach a 2-2 load per academic year that would entail a combination of general education courses and religious studies courses at all levels, including rotation in our undergraduate theories and methods course for departmental majors and our graduate-level methods course for masters students in religious studies and related programs. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Required Qualifications : Candidates will have a Ph.D. in the academic study of religion or a closely related field from an accredited institution of higher learning. Candidates will have an exceptional record of scholarly accomplishment with significant national and/or international stature that is commensurate with a tenured appointment in religious studies. Candidates will demonstrate interest in interdisciplinary research and teaching that fosters engagement with departments in the college and across the university. Candidates will have a record of success in teaching, advising, and mentoring students of a wide range of backgrounds in higher education and will be familiar with experiential and engaged pedagogies that can contribute to the success of University of North Carolina at Charlotte students. Candidates will be an advocate for the study of religion and the humanities and have a scholarly agenda with a public-facing dimension capable of attracting the interest of and engaging the Charlotte community. Preferred Qualifications: The department is especially interested in candidates whose specialization lies in the study of critical comparative scriptures, that is, the use of historical and cultural analysis to engage religious texts and communities in order to better understand the dynamics of their functions, roles, interpretations, and receptions. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The Department of Religious Studies at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for the Blumenthal Professorship in Religious Studies, a full-time, tenured faculty position to begin 16 August 2027. The appointment will be made at the associate or full professor rank. Applicants should apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position #5853) and submit the following materials: 1) a letter of interest discussing the applicant’s professional qualifications and history regarding the five requirements and how the applicant would contribute to the department, college, and university’s research, teaching, and service mission; and 2) a curriculum vitae. Applicants selected for further consideration will be asked to send three confidential letters of recommendation to the search committee chair. Finalists will also be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage and include all UNC -Charlotte students while eliminating barriers to learning, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The candidate selected for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check and will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Sean McCloud, Chair, Department of Religious Studies ( spmcclou@charlotte.edu ). Job Open Date 10/13/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/13/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 Contact Information for References 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71170": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants are required to submit the following documents: One-page letter of interest Curriculum Vitae Research statement (up to three pages) detailing areas of expertise and research vision Teaching statement (up to two pages) Contact information for at least three references Position Information General Information Position Number 001837 Working Title Assistant Professor Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College College of Engineering (Col) Department Department of Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science ( MEES ) is the largest department within the William States Lee College of Engineering, staffed by 54 full-time faculty members and 16 staff members who serve about 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. There are six active NSF CAREER awardees, and several faculty are recognized as Fellows of leading professional societies. The MEES department is internationally recognized for research programs in advanced manufacturing, precision metrology, battery research, motorsports/automotive engineering, control systems, computational mechanics, as well as emerging areas of research focus such as hypersonics and autonomous vehicles. The Department is home to the Center for Precision Metrology, the Center for Additive Manufacturing of Advanced Ceramics and the Battery Complexity, Autonomous Vehicle and Electrification Research Center. The Department has also recently launched a popular undergraduate concentration in Aerospace Engineering. The College of Engineering is home to the newly established NSF Grid Modernization Engine in the Carolinas and is leading initiatives in several areas of strategic research focus for the university, including Advanced Manufacturing and Metrology, Transformational Energy, and Transportation and Advanced Mobility. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Department of Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte is currently seeking exceptionally qualified candidates for a 9-month tenure-track Assistant Professor position with research expertise in solid mechanics and mechanics of materials, and an expected start date of August 2027. Specific areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Mechanics of materials in extreme environments Nonlinear, multiscale, and multiphysics mechanics Mechanics of advanced composites, architected and multifunctional materials, and adaptive structures Structural integrity, reliability, and predictive lifetime modeling. Candidates working at the intersection of mechanics and emerging technologies are particularly encouraged to apply, including those developing scientific machine learning, quantum computing algorithms, advanced numerical methods, and full-field or in-situ experimental techniques. Relevant applications may include aerospace and hypersonic structures, energy systems, materials for nuclear and fusion technologies, and materials and structures subjected to extreme loading or environmental conditions. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in one of the following areas: Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science, Aerospace Engineering, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Applicants should be committed to teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. It is expected that the successful candidate will build strong collaborations with industry partners and national laboratories, publish in leading research journals, and establish a strong externally funded research program. Applicants should also be committed to supervising undergraduate capstone projects and graduate theses and dissertations. Priority will be given to candidates with strong research records, as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications, externally funded research, and a plan for future research that aligns with the university’s and department’s research focus in this area. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Applicants are required to submit the following documents: One-page letter of interest Curriculum Vitae Research statement (up to three pages) detailing areas of expertise and research vision Teaching statement (up to two pages) Contact information for at least three references Job Open Date 09/28/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/11/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Contact Information for References Research Interest Statement Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71149": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English in African American and/or African Diaspora Literature",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor of English in African American and/or African Diaspora Literature Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor of English in African American and/or African Diaspora Literature Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a position of Assistant Professor of English specializing in African American and/or African Diaspora literature, beginning August 2027. The appointment is a 9-month tenure-track position. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. Please apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu with the following materials: a letter of application; curriculum vitae; a research statement; a teaching statement that addresses how the candidate’s teaching practices engage all students and eliminate barriers to learning; a writing sample of no more than 25 pages; unofficial transcripts; and contact information for three references. Preliminary interviews will be conducted via Zoom. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. The candidate chosen for this position will subject to a criminal background check. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Screening of applicants will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled; applications received before November 16 will be prioritized. Please direct inquiries to the Chair of the Search Committee, Professor Lara Vetter, at LVetter@charlotte.edu. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 005287 Working Title Assistant Professor of English in African American and/or African Diaspora Literature Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department English Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of English, with more than 30 full-time faculty and approximately 250 majors and 60 graduate students, offers BA and MA degrees with area emphases in Creative Writing, Literature and Culture, Children’s Literature, Linguistics, English Education, and Professional/Technical Writing. The department is housed in the College of Humanities and Earth and Social Sciences ( CHESS ). As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, CHESS houses 15 departments, 18 interdisciplinary programs, and 6 applied centers, offering a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 university, and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities Assistant Professors are invested in working closely with and mentoring English undergraduate and graduate students, and fully participating in service to the English Department, with opportunities to serve the institution at large. We are also interested in candidates who can work collaboratively with faculty and students from a wide range of disciplines, cultures, and academic backgrounds. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. Teaching for this position may include special topics courses for B.A. and M.A. students, honors seminars, surveys, and general education courses. The teaching load for research-intensive faculty is 2/2. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience By the time of appointment, the successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. in English (during the selection process, ABD candidates will be required to submit a letter from their dissertation advisor guaranteeing completion of the degree by the time of appointment). Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a position of Assistant Professor of English specializing in African American and/or African Diaspora literature, beginning August 2027. The appointment is a 9-month tenure-track position. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. Please apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu with the following materials: a letter of application; curriculum vitae; a research statement; a teaching statement that addresses how the candidate’s teaching practices engage all students and eliminate barriers to learning; a writing sample of no more than 25 pages; unofficial transcripts; and contact information for three references. Preliminary interviews will be conducted via Zoom. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. The candidate chosen for this position will subject to a criminal background check. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Screening of applicants will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled; applications received before November 16 will be prioritized. Please direct inquiries to the Chair of the Search Committee, Professor Lara Vetter, at LVetter@charlotte.edu. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/09/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/16/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Research Interest Statement Teaching Philosophy Writing Sample Unofficial Transcripts Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
