    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71101": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor and Director of Forensics",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor and Director of Forensics Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor and Director of Forensics Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The Department of Communications Studies at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invited applications for a Director of Forensics and a Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor rank, 9-month teaching-track position beginning August 16, 2027. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The review of applications will begin October 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants must apply electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu/ , position #005250. Please upload a cover letter responding to the qualifications listed above, a current CV, and contact information for three references. Contact Dr. Nance Riffe (nriffe@charlotte.edu, Search Committee Chair) or Dr. Erin Basinger (ebasinge@charlotte.edu, Chair of the Department of Communication Studies) with questions. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest earned degree upon hire. For all open-rank positions, rank is dependent on education and experience. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 005250 Working Title Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor and Director of Forensics Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department Communication Studies Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Communication Studies (https://communication.charlotte.edu/) has 53 full-time faculty with areas of emphasis in health communication; organizational communication; public relations; media and technology studies; rhetoric, culture, and social change; and writing, rhetoric and digital studies. The department serves approximately 870 Communication Studies majors and minors and 25 Master’s students. The department’s primary mission is to provide high quality instruction to its undergraduate and graduate students and to contribute significantly to the understanding of communication issues as they affect people’s lives, especially in the Charlotte metropolitan region. Through its teaching, scholarship and service the department is committed to serving diverse populations. The Department of Communication Studies is part of the College of Humanities & Social Sciences ( CHESS ). As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, CHESS houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte (https://www.charlotte.edu/) is a doctoral, research-intensive urban university, located on an expanding modern campus. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 university, and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities The duties of the Director of Forensics include, but are not limited to: Arranging and supervising tournament travel, student organization registration and compliance, budget management, and supervision of graduate coaching assistants. Supporting the growth and health of UNC Charlotte Speech through recruitment and retention efforts, including outreach to high schools and other relevant organizations, as well as on-campus recruitment events. Recruiting and retaining undergraduate speech team competitors and graduate coaching assistants. Providing high-quality and pedagogically-sound coaching and instruction for students in competitive intercollegiate speech and individual events including weekly team meetings, preparing students for tournaments, and engaging in necessary pre-season preparation. Along with: Directing the University of North Carolina at Charlotte’s competitive intercollegiate forensics team Teaching courses in the theory, research, and practice of communication studies Working effectively with a diverse student population of undergraduate and graduate students Service at the department, college, and/or university levels, as appropriate The candidate will be expected to teach a minimum of three undergraduate courses per semester in addition to their duties as Director of Forensics, as staffing needs dictate, including foundational courses (e.g., Introduction to Public Speaking ; Introduction to Interpersonal Communication; Introduction to Communication Studies ) and other topics in the candidate’s area of specialty. Successful candidates will teach undergraduate students in the Department of Communication Studies and, depending on interest and qualifications, may teach Master’s students in the department’s graduate program. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Required Qualifications: Required qualifications include an M.A. or Ph.D. in Communication Studies or a related area. Preferred Qualifications: We welcome applications from candidates with interests in any of the concentration areas in the department: health communication ; organizational communication ; media and technology studies; rhetoric, culture, and social change; and/or public relations. Candidates with a background in performance studies, argumentation, and/or rhetoric and who have previous experience coaching Individual events are preferred. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The Department of Communications Studies at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invited applications for a Director of Forensics and a Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor rank, 9-month teaching-track position beginning August 16, 2027. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The review of applications will begin October 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants must apply electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu/ , position #005250. Please upload a cover letter responding to the qualifications listed above, a current CV, and contact information for three references. Contact Dr. Nance Riffe (nriffe@charlotte.edu, Search Committee Chair) or Dr. Erin Basinger (ebasinge@charlotte.edu, Chair of the Department of Communication Studies) with questions. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest earned degree upon hire. For all open-rank positions, rank is dependent on education and experience. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/07/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/30/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Contact Dr. Nance Riffe (nriffe@charlotte.edu, Search Committee Chair) or Dr. Erin Basinger ebasinge@charlotte.edu, Chair of the Department of Communication Studies) with questions. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Contact Information for References Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Optional Documents Other Document 2 Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71099": {
      "title": "Visiting 3D Lecturer/Technician in Ceramics",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Visiting 3D Lecturer/Technician in Ceramics Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Visiting 3D Lecturer/Technician in Ceramics Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Information General Information Position Number 005029 Working Title Visiting 3D Lecturer/Technician in Ceramics Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Department Art & Art History Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Art & Art History empowers students to broaden their creative perspectives through rigorous engagement with Art, Design, Art History, and Art Education. Our programs cultivate innovative studio methods, scholarly research, and arts pedagogy, operating on the premise that art and visual culture are vital to our academic community and broader society. Currently, the department awards a BFA in Graphic Design, a BA in Art (with an Art Education concentration), and a BFA in Art featuring six concentration areas, alongside an Art History minor and an Honors Program in Art History. Furthermore, our faculty support the broader University General Education program. The B.F.A. in Art with a concentration in 3D Interdisciplinary Studies: Art in Space brings together Ceramics, Digital Fabrication, Fibers, and Sculpture to offer a contemporary and flexible approach to making art in three dimensions. This area exposes students to a mix of materials—including clay, fabric, wood, metal, and 3D-printed components—to create functional pottery, sculptural forms, installation environments, wearable pieces, and digitally fabricated structures. Introductory courses focus on materials and techniques, while advanced courses support self-directed projects and the development of a personal artistic voice. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Responsibilities of this lecturer position include teaching undergraduate university courses and maintaining the Ceramics lab facilities. Assigned courses may include Ceramics Wheel 1 and 2, Handbuilding, and 3D Foundations. This position may, at times, be assigned to teach advanced Ceramics and beginning-level Sculpture courses. The candidate is expected to understand interdisciplinary trends in contemporary ceramics. In addition to teaching, the person in this position will manage a comprehensive ceramics studio. Duties will include: budgeting and purchasing; kiln firing; equipment maintenance; maintaining and enforcing safety protocols; hiring and coordinating work-study students; and providing technical assistance for ceramic faculty. Starts August 16, 2027. Salary and start up package commensurate with qualifications. The nine-month contract includes 4 courses per year (2/2 course load) with potential for summer teaching for additional compensation. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Minimum Education/Experience Requirements: An MFA in the teaching discipline or a related discipline is required by the date of appointment. Candidates must have a minimum of two semesters of documented college-level teaching experience with an emphasis in ceramics. Graduate teaching experience as an instructor of record can count toward this requirement. Additional Desired Qualifications: The ability to teach Ceramics Wheel 1 and 2, Handbuilding, and 3D Foundations An understanding of interdisciplinary trends in contemporary ceramics and ability to teach interdisciplinary courses. Ability to manage a comprehensive ceramics studio, including: budgeting and purchasing, equipment maintenance, kiln firing and safety, and coordination of work-study students. Ability to teach additional courses, such as: kiln building, glaze and material research, mold making, digital fabrication, and ceramic design. Competence working in a comprehensive 3d studio that includes a woodshop, metal shop, and foundry While Lecturers are not evaluated on research practice, a record of studio accomplishment, engagement with professional organizations, and an active research agenda are highly valued. Evidence of effective communication skills; collaboration; working supportively in a professional learning community. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Job Open Date 10/07/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/02/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Unofficial Transcripts Contact Information for References Other Document Teaching Philosophy Online Portfolio Other Document 2 Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71061": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Richardson",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Richardson Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Richardson Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants looking to graduate with their PhD/MD within the 2026 year are welcome to apply. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number POST40 Working Title Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Richardson Division Academic Affairs Department College of Science (Col) Work Unit Biological Sciences Work Location Woodward Hall Vacancy Open To All Candidates Position Designation Post Doc Employment Type Temporary - Full-time Hours per week 40hr Work Schedule 8am – 5pm Pay Rate Pay Rate varies Minimum Experience/Education The Postdoctoral appointee must have recently (within the last eight years) been awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.) Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education: Required Qualifications: The postdoctoral appointee must have: Ph.D. in Biological Sciences, Biochemistry, or related discipline Previous experience in laboratory setting and protocol development Demonstrated evidence of publications in the field and excellent presentation skills. Preferred Qualifications: Good data management, analytical and computer skills Ability to design, execute and write up experimental work independently Good written and verbal communication skills Ability to work to deadlines Proven ability to work effectively and responsibly without close supervision Experience in lipid or other nanoparticle formulations with loaded and conjugated payloads will be particularly considered Experience in genomics/transcriptomics sample prep, equipment usage, and analysis will be particularly considered Experience using flow cytometry and related fluorescence bio-imaging techniques with ability to maintain equipment and train new users Commitment to own continuous professional development particularly in regard to knowledge and applications of flow cytometry and related bioimaging equipment usage and analysis techniques Duties and Responsibilities The Postdoctoral Fellow will work under the supervision of a regular faculty member and Chair of the department, who serve as a mentor to the Fellow. The Postdoctoral Fellow will work to support ongoing projects in the laboratory, and support additional maintenance, projects and training needs of flow cytometry and Imagestream for the department. Opportunities to publish the results of research or scholarship during the period of appointment. As a Postdoctoral Research Fellow serving the Richardson lab and providing technical support to the department flow cytometry equipment, main duties will include: · Maintain stocks, resources, and equipment in the lab and related equipment in department used by the lab, as appropriate · Keep informed of recent advances in the field · Conduct and supervise junior researchers in a program of investigation along with the supervisor/principal investigator, as appropriate · Evaluate methods and techniques used and results obtained by other researchers and to relate such evaluations appropriately to their own work · Communicate or present research results through publication or other recognized forms of output · Contribute to the supervision of junior researchers, as appropriate · Maintain own continuing professional development and act as a mentor to less experienced colleagues, as appropriate · Maintain a safe work environment, including ensuring compliance with legislation and undertaking of risk assessments · Use, maintain, and train others in the department flow cytometry and related bioimaging fluidics based equipment · Analyze and interpret flow cytometry and bioimaging fluidics based results and data related to projects in the lab · Maintain autoclaves for compliant usage Other Work/Responsibilities Necessary Licenses or Certifications Proposed Hire Date 11/16/2026 Contact Information Expected Length of Assignment 1 year minimum Posting Open Date 10/07/2026 Posting Close Date Special Notes to Applicants Applicants looking to graduate with their PhD/MD within the 2026 year are welcome to apply. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Contact Information for References Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Curriculum Vitae 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
