    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70972": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The successful candidate will be expected to: ● Establish a strong, externally funded research program; ● Publish in leading research journals; ● Teach undergraduate and graduate courses; ● Advise graduate theses and dissertations; ● Supervise undergraduate capstone projects; and ● Develop collaborations with faculty, industry partners, and national laboratories Applicants must submit the following materials for Position 004407: 1. A one-page letter of interest; 2. A curriculum vitae; 3. A research statement of up to three pages describing the applicant’s expertise and research vision; 4. A teaching statement of up to two pages; and 5. Contact information for at least three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 11, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Initial interviews are expected to begin in December 2026. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 004407 Working Title Assistant Professor Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College College of Engineering (Col) Department Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science ( MEES ) is the largest department in the William States Lee College of Engineering. Its 54 full-time faculty members and 16 staff members serve approximately 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The department currently has six active NSF CAREER awards, and several faculty members are Fellows of leading professional societies. MEES has established research programs in advanced manufacturing, precision metrology, battery research, motorsports and automotive engineering, control systems, computational mechanics, hypersonics, transport phenomena, and autonomous vehicles. The department is home to the Center for Precision Metrology, the Center for Additive Manufacturing of Advanced Ceramics, and the Battery Complexity, Autonomous Vehicle and Electrification Research Center. It has also recently launched an undergraduate concentration in Aerospace Engineering. The College of Engineering leads initiatives in several strategic research areas, including Advanced Manufacturing and Metrology, Transformational Energy, and Transportation and Advanced Mobility. The College is also home to the newly established NSF Grid Modernization Engine in the Carolinas. Other University initiatives supporting robotics and unmanned systems include the Charlotte Aviation Innovation and Research Institute ( AIR ) and the National Defense and Intelligence Innovation Institute (NDI3). Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Department of Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in robotics, with an expected start date of August 2027. We seek candidates conducting foundational research in robotics and autonomy with broad applications across manufacturing, aerospace, space, defense, and energy. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Embodied AI, physical intelligence, and learning-enabled robotics; Robotic manipulation and locomotion; Mobile robotics and autonomous systems; Robotics and automation for advanced manufacturing; and Soft, continuum, and compliant robotic systems. Research that complements and expands existing departmental strengths is particularly encouraged. Relevant faculty expertise includes digital twins and AI for manufacturing, human–robot interaction, assisted driving, traffic-flow automation, bio-inspired robotics, multi-robot systems and swarms, precision engineering, and path planning for unmanned aerial, marine, and space systems. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering, Robotics Engineering, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Priority will be given to candidates with strong research records, as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications, relevant professional activities, evidence of or potential for securing external funding, and a research vision aligned with departmental and University priorities. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The successful candidate will be expected to: ● Establish a strong, externally funded research program; ● Publish in leading research journals; ● Teach undergraduate and graduate courses; ● Advise graduate theses and dissertations; ● Supervise undergraduate capstone projects; and ● Develop collaborations with faculty, industry partners, and national laboratories Applicants must submit the following materials for Position 004407: 1. A one-page letter of interest; 2. A curriculum vitae; 3. A research statement of up to three pages describing the applicant’s expertise and research vision; 4. A teaching statement of up to two pages; and 5. Contact information for at least three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 11, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Initial interviews are expected to begin in December 2026. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/02/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/11/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/01/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Research Interest Statement Statement of Research, Teaching & Leadership Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70935": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Early Revolutionary America",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Teaching Professor in Early Revolutionary America Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Teaching Professor in Early Revolutionary America Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications received by October 23, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted to https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position number #005580) and should include a cover letter, a CV, a two-page sample syllabus, an assignment from one course, and the names of three references. Finalists will be asked for three letters of recommendation. Please direct any questions about this position to Dr. Sonya Ramsey, chair of the search committee, at sramse17@charlotte.edu. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check and will be asked to submit their Official Transcript of their highest earned degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 005580 Working Title Assistant Teaching Professor in Early Revolutionary America Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department History Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department This position will have its home in the History Department. The department employs 30 full-time and 5 part-time faculty members. Degree programs include the B.A. in History (approximately 175 students) and the M.A. in History (approximately 35 students). We offer public, digital, and applied history courses at the undergraduate and graduate level, and the department’s MA program features a special concentration in Public History. The department also works closely with other interdisciplinary departments and programs across the College, including Africana Studies, Latin American Studies, Capitalism Studies, Women and Gender Studies, Human-Centered AI, Global Studies, Religious Studies, Legal Studies, Health and Medical Humanities, and American Studies. It also houses several journals and professional associations, including the Journal of Urban History and French Historical Studies. As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 university and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Within a 4-4 teaching load, the successful candidate will teach courses serving history majors and minors, a US History course in the general education program, and will contribute to service to the department, college, and university. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Required qualifications: Applicants are required to have a Ph.D. in History, expertise in Early /Revolutionary America. Teaching experience at the collegiate level, and the ability to teach undergraduate courses to support the History Department’s undergraduate curriculum, and its major and minor. Desired qualifications: We are especially interested in applications from historians with teaching expertise and skills in one or more of the following areas: Indigenous History; the US Revolutionary War/Early Republic Era to 1830; the Atlantic World; and Public, Digital, and Applied History. We are also interested in a candidate’s potential contributions to thematically or subject-related interdisciplinary studies departments and programs. Successful candidates will demonstrate teaching excellence and use effective instructional strategies that include: the design and implementation of innovative curricula; advancing strategies to help students develop AI literacy and human-centered, ethical AI research skills; demonstrating dynamic faculty/student classroom communication and interaction; and providing examples of supporting undergraduate student engagement and achievement efforts in a non-classroom setting. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Applications received by October 23, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted to https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position number #005580) and should include a cover letter, a CV, a two-page sample syllabus, an assignment from one course, and the names of three references. Finalists will be asked for three letters of recommendation. Please direct any questions about this position to Dr. Sonya Ramsey, chair of the search committee, at sramse17@charlotte.edu. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check and will be asked to submit their Official Transcript of their highest earned degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/01/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/23/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/11/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Sample Syllabi Other Document Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 2 Other Document 3 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
