    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70794": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application. Only electronic submissions will be accepted. For more information, regarding the position, and to apply electronically, go to https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position #003071). Please include the following documents with your electronic submission: A cover letter (not to exceed three pages) that addresses your interest in the position, and in teaching in a Distance Education program. Additionally, your cover letter must address each of the four required criteria listed previously. Letters that do not address the four required criteria will not be considered complete. Documentation of excellence in teaching. Please provide course syllabi and student evaluations for your most recent two years of teaching where available. In lieu of (or in addition to) student evaluations, peer evaluations and/or annual performance reviews of teaching by a supervisor may be submitted as documentation. These and any other indices of teaching excellence (e.g., teaching awards, recognitions, etc.,) should be discussed in the cover letter. Applicants without student evaluations or other documentation should discuss their potential for teaching excellence in their cover letter. A current and complete curriculum vita. Names and contact information for at least three references. Candidates selected for the initial round of Zoom interviews will be asked to provide three reference letters. Finalists who are invited for an on-campus interview will be asked to conduct a classroom teaching demonstration, and to separately discuss/demonstrate their approach to online teaching. Finalists will also be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire, and will be subject to a Criminal Background Check. For additional information, please contact Dr. Lyn Exum ( Lexum@charlotte.edu ), Chair of the Search Committee. Review of applications will begin on October 28, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Position Information General Information Position Number 003071 Working Title Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department Criminal Justice and Criminology Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department. The Department consists of fifteen tenure-track faculty members and four full-time lecturers/teaching professors. The B.A. in Criminal Justice (approximately 680 majors) is offered in-person as well as online. The Department also offers minors in Criminal Justice and in Crime Analytics (approximately 150 students combined), as well as an M.S. in Criminal Justice (approximately 35 graduate students). Additionally, the Department contributes coursework to the undergraduate minor in Legal Studies, the Interdisciplinary Ph.D. Program in Public Policy, and to the School of Data Science. The College. As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. The University. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 university, and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a full-time, on-campus position as a Lecturer or Teaching Professor specializing in Distance Education in Criminal Justice and Criminology. The rank is open to Lecturer or Assistant Teaching Professor, and is dependent upon the applicant’s education and experience. The start date is August 16, 2027. This is a non-tenure track position. The position has an initial appointment of three years with the possibility of continued reappointments depending upon job performance. The workload of a Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor primarily involves teaching undergraduate students (usually four course sections per semester, some of which will be online), but also includes service to the department, college, university, and/or community. This position will assist in the delivery of the Department’s newly launched 100% online undergraduate degree program in Criminal Justice. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience There are four required qualifications for the position : (1) a Master’s or Ph.D. in Criminal Justice or Criminology (or a related discipline) or a J.D. at the time of appointment, (2) experience in teaching undergraduate courses across the discipline of Criminal Justice/Criminology at a two-year or four-year college, (3) experience or extensive training in teaching online courses at a two-year or four-year college, and (4) evidence of (or the potential for) teaching excellence. Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience teaching courses related to police, courts, corrections, data analytics and/or the responsible use and impact of artificial intelligence in criminal justice is preferred. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Application. Only electronic submissions will be accepted. For more information, regarding the position, and to apply electronically, go to https://jobs.charlotte.edu (position #003071). Please include the following documents with your electronic submission: A cover letter (not to exceed three pages) that addresses your interest in the position, and in teaching in a Distance Education program. Additionally, your cover letter must address each of the four required criteria listed previously. Letters that do not address the four required criteria will not be considered complete. Documentation of excellence in teaching. Please provide course syllabi and student evaluations for your most recent two years of teaching where available. In lieu of (or in addition to) student evaluations, peer evaluations and/or annual performance reviews of teaching by a supervisor may be submitted as documentation. These and any other indices of teaching excellence (e.g., teaching awards, recognitions, etc.,) should be discussed in the cover letter. Applicants without student evaluations or other documentation should discuss their potential for teaching excellence in their cover letter. A current and complete curriculum vita. Names and contact information for at least three references. Candidates selected for the initial round of Zoom interviews will be asked to provide three reference letters. Finalists who are invited for an on-campus interview will be asked to conduct a classroom teaching demonstration, and to separately discuss/demonstrate their approach to online teaching. Finalists will also be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire, and will be subject to a Criminal Background Check. For additional information, please contact Dr. Lyn Exum ( Lexum@charlotte.edu ), Chair of the Search Committee. Review of applications will begin on October 28, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Job Open Date 09/28/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/28/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 Other Document 3 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
