      "title": "Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; Fire &amp; Occupational Safety",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; Fire &amp; Occupational Safety Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; Fire & Occupational Safety Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. All finalists will be required to provide an official transcript. All finalists will be subject to a criminal background check. Position Information General Information Position Number EPTF50 Working Title Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; Fire & Occupational Safety Division Academic Affairs Department College of Engineering (Col) Work Unit Albert School of Construction Work Location Albert School of Construction Vacancy Open To All Candidates Position Designation Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty Employment Type Temporary - Part-time Hours per week Varies Work Schedule Varies Pay Rate Pay Rate varies Minimum Experience/Education Faculty teaching undergraduate courses: doctorate or master’s degree in the teaching discipline or master’s degree with a minimum of 18 graduate semester hours in the teaching discipline. Faculty teaching graduate courses: earned doctorate / terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the highest earned degree in the discipline. UNC Charlotte also considers competence, effectiveness, and capacity, including, as appropriate, undergraduate and graduate degrees, related work experiences in the field, professional licensure and certifications, honors and awards, continuous documented excellence in teaching, or other demonstrated competencies and achievements that contribute to effective teaching and student learning outcomes. Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education: Duties and Responsibilities UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits. Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations. Academic Programs include: Fire & Safety Engineering Technology – Fire & Safety Concentration (Undergraduate) Fire & Safety Engineering Technology – Occupational Safety Concentration (Undergraduate) Fire Protection and Safety Management (Graduate) The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check. Other Work/Responsibilities Necessary Licenses or Certifications Proposed Hire Date 01/04/2027 Contact Information Fire & Safety Concentration – David Newell (dlnewell@charltote.edu) Occupational Safety Concentration – Michael Benjamin (mbanjam3@charlotte.edu) Fire Protection and Safety Management – Michael Benjamin (mbanjam3@charlotte.edu) Expected Length of Assignment Spring 2027 Term and/or Summer 2027 Term Posting Open Date 10/05/2026 Posting Close Date 04/30/2027 Special Notes to Applicants All finalists will be required to provide an official transcript. All finalists will be subject to a criminal background check. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Optional Documents Other Document Unofficial Transcripts Contact Information for References Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70674": {
      "title": "Department Chair &amp; Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Department Chair &amp; Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Department Chair & Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This posting is open to UNC Charlotte employees only. Position Information General Information Position Number 005119 Working Title Department Chair & Professor Classification Title Department Chair/ Director College Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Department Theatre Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 12 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Theatre inspires students to expand their vision of the world and themselves through the study and practice of theatre. The Department offers a B.A. in Theatre with concentrations available in design and production, performance, or theatre education. Our student-centered, community-engaged education in theatre fosters the development of “cross-trained artists” who are prepared to participate in professional theatre as well as any profession that places value on imaginative thinking, collaboration, and communication skills. The program creates intellectual and creative experiences for students through rigorous classroom instruction and innovative approaches to stage production. The faculty comprises nationally recognized scholars and artists who mentor students through individual projects as well as collaborative works that embrace a wide range of aesthetic perspectives, and innovative creative research practices. Vacancy Open to UNC Charlotte Employees Only Description of Work Responsible for the daily operation of the department and the department programs. The Department Chair/ Director will promote excellence in department programs within the University. Chair/ Director responsibilities are classified as professional duties requiring leadership and mentoring of faculty, administrative expertise to include vision, long term and short-term planning, decision-making and coordination of instruction. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Chair reports to the Dean of the CoA+A, sets Departmental priorities in consultation with the faculty, provides leadership in strategic planning, administers Departmental and College policies, and assumes final responsibility for Departmental and production budgets, planning documents, self‐studies, and annual reports. The Chair takes an active role in, and assumes final responsibility for hiring all faculty and administrative staff. The Chair is responsible for assigning faculty workloads, individual course schedules, service assignments, annual reviews of all full time faculty, and provides guidance in RPT , PTR and other processes. The Chair also assumes final responsibility for the production season schedules and works collaboratively with the Associate Dean for Production Arts Operations on production support and the use of performance spaces. The Chair represents the Department to the Dean and the Provost & Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs, and serves as a liaison to the arts community beyond the University. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Successful candidates will display an energetic and collaborative leadership style that inspires faculty, students, and staff to develop a shared vision for the future of the Department of Theatre. Candidates are sought who demonstrate leadership in 21st century approaches to Theatre, evidence of leadership in advancing innovation in pedagogy, performance, research, partnership building, administration, and in the workplace. Successful candidates will also possess excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to recruit, mentor, and support faculty and students of many different backgrounds. Successful candidates may have experience with community-based, site-specific, devised, and/or collaborative work, and an understanding and appreciation for the variety of approaches, perspectives, and specializations that comprise research in Theatre. This may include an ability to advocate for the value of research in and resources for Theatre, in all its forms, relative to UNC Charlotte’s unique role as an R1 Urban Research University. Candidates may also have qualifications that include arts administration, fundraising, and artistic or managerial experience in professional theatre. This is a tenured position; eligibility for tenure rank (Associate or Full Professor) will depend on the background and experience of the candidate. Necessary Licenses/Certifications The successful candidate will hold a terminal degree in Theatre or related field and have a record of significant professional activity suitable for appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor, as well as a proven record of academic and professional leadership. Special Notes to Applicants This posting is open to UNC Charlotte employees only. Job Open Date 09/23/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/15/2026 Proposed Hire Date 07/01/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70635": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Art Education",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor of Art Education Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor of Art Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Information General Information Position Number 005283 Working Title Assistant Professor of Art Education Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Department Art & Art History Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Art & Art History empowers students to expand their vision through study and practice in Art, Design, Art History, and Art Education. We foster innovative studio practices, scholarship, and arts-based teaching, affirming that art and visual culture are essential to our students, university, communities, and future. The Department currently offers a Bachelor of Fine Arts (B.F.A.) in Graphic Design, a Bachelor of Arts (B.A.) in Art and Art Education, and a Bachelor of Fine Arts (B.F.A.) in Art with six concentrations, along with a minor in Art History and an Honors Program in Art History. Additionally, the Department contributes to the University General Education curriculum. The Art Education concentration prepares teacher candidates to earn their NC licensure in Art K-12 through a rigorous professional curriculum with content and pedagogy that fosters a love of teaching and learning. The Art Education concentration seeks a colleague to enhance and advance the department’s record of innovation through dynamic, engaged teaching, impactful research, and visionary, collegial leadership and service. Faculty members in the Art Education concentration collaborate with colleagues in the studio concentrations; art history; university galleries; the Cato College of Education ( COED ), the Teacher Education Advising and Licensure office ( TEAL ), and the Office of School and Community Partnerships ( OSCP ). Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The Department of Art & Art History is seeking a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor in Art Education. The successful candidate will be an educator, artist, and researcher who will contribute to growing the Art & Art History Department’s B.A. in Art, concentration in Art Education and its Graduate Certificate in Education, concentration in Art Education. Teaching: The successful candidate will design, revise, and teach art education theory and methods courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The Assistant Professor of Art Education will be assigned a 3/2 teaching load, encompassing a broad range of courses that include online and face-to-face classes, as well as supervision and observation of student teachers. Research: Successful candidates will demonstrate an active and ongoing research agenda in the field of art education that is disseminated through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, funding, community projects, and/or exhibitions. Service: The successful candidate will serve as the Area Coordinator for Art Education, contributing to the ongoing development of the Art Education concentrations through curriculum revision, student mentoring, and assessment. They will serve as a liaison with the Cato College of Education and serve on Department, College, and University committees. Starts August 16, 2027. Salary and start up package commensurate with qualifications. The nine-month contract includes 5 courses per year (3/2 course load) with potential for summer teaching for additional compensation. Hiring is subject to budget availability. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Minimum Education/Experience Requirements: Credentials: A terminal degree (PhD or EdD) in Art Education or related field from an accredited institution of higher education is required and must be conferred by August 2027. Eligible to meet state licensure requirements for teachers, Art K-12 Prior experience teaching in an Art Education college/university curriculum. Demonstrated commitment to working with K-12 schools with a minimum of three school years of successful Visual Arts teaching in K-12 settings Demonstrated ability to apply knowledge of contemporary issues in education, contemporary art and art history, classroom teaching as both practice and research, technology, and student assessments to support teaching. Demonstrated ability to implement a peer-reviewed research agenda in art education through scholarly publications; regional, national, and/or international presentations; peer reviewed exhibitions; research funding; and/or community-based creative projects. A track record of effective leadership and communication skills, with the demonstrated ability to work supportively and collaboratively as part of a professional learning community that encompasses students, colleagues, and community partners. Demonstrated ability to contribute to the profession of Art Education on the local/regional, state, national, and/or international level through involvement with community organizations, professional networks ( NAEA , InSEA, etc.), and/or academic journals (ie. as a peer reviewer). Additional Desired Qualifications A BFA , MA, or MFA degree in Studio Art from an accredited institution of higher education. Experience supporting teacher candidates in practicum and clinical settings, including student teaching. Experience supporting teacher candidates through assessment processes using established instruments, including edTPA and CPAST . Demonstrated ability to apply state and national standards governing teacher education programs and teacher licensure to candidate assessment and/or program development. Established research agenda in art education with significant accomplishments in publications, presentations, exhibitions, research funding, and/or community-based creative projects. Demonstrated ability to serve in shared governance of college/university policy and procedure (i.e., committee work). Demonstrated ability to develop college-level curriculum and/or conduct assessment reviews at the college level. Screening will begin November 1, 2026. Position open until filled. Applicants are required to complete an electronic application. For electronic application: please go to jobs.charlotte.edu; the Position Designation is Faculty; and the Position Number is #005283. Please prepare each of the following documents to be submitted and uploaded individually in PDF format: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter addressing the position duties and requirements 1-Page Research Statement that highlights area(s) of research interest, significant current or past research projects, methodology, and directions for future research. 1-Page Teaching Statement that highlights values and beliefs that inform teaching in art education, pedagogical approaches, and classroom practice.. Unofficial Graduate Transcripts for the highest degree earned. Contact information for three references. Please note that applicants will be subject to a criminal background check. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application, all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Job Open Date 09/21/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/01/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Unofficial Transcripts Statement of Research, Teaching & Leadership Contact Information for References Research Interest Statement Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
