    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71457": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow for NIH R01 SKyRoCKeT Study",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Postdoctoral Research Fellow for NIH R01 SKyRoCKeT Study Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Postdoctoral Research Fellow for NIH R01 SKyRoCKeT Study Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application materials must be submitted through jobs.charlotte.edu and should include: · Resume/CV · Cover letter · Contact information for 2 references · Unofficial Transcripts · Note: This position will not be able to sponsor a visa, including for any possible renewal term. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number POST40 Working Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow for NIH R01 SKyRoCKeT Study Division Academic Affairs Department College of Health & Human Svc (Col) Work Unit Public Hlth & Hlth Admin Work Location CHHS & Belk Gym Vacancy Open To All Candidates Position Designation Post Doc Employment Type Temporary - Full-time Hours per week 40 hours/week Work Schedule The postdoc will contribute 40 hours per week to the responsibilities as directed by their primary supervisor, Dr. Laura Gunn (PI), as well as their broader supervisory team including Dr. Daheia Barr-Anderson (PI), and Dr. Trudy Moore-Harrison (Co-I). SKyRoCKeT involves interdisciplinary team-based public health science research. Responsibilities and scheduling may vary from week to week depending on data management and analysis needs, as well as possible data collection needs and involvement throughout this position. Therefore, it is the responsibility of the postdoc to communicate and confirm their schedule and expectations regularly among the team. Because the study is focused on walking behavior in 6-17 year-olds who are in school during weekdays, then the postdoc may also be expected to participate in study recruitment events on-campus or in the community, as well as work later afternoons/early evenings and/or weekends when data collection with study participants is needed, adjusting their time off during the week accordingly when/if involved in data collection and/or recruitment. Pay Rate $58,215 Minimum Experience/Education Required Qualifications · Completed doctoral degree in Statistics, Data Science, Exercise Science, Kinesiology, Biostatistics, Epidemiology, Public Health, or closely related areas · Analytic coding skills for data management and analyses in R statistical software · Attention to detail · Interest in learning about and applying quantitative methods in exercise science to inform public health physical activity guidelines · Collegiality within an interdisciplinary team; work well collaboratively & independently · Strong intellectual curiosity · Work flexibility with some availability in later afternoons/evenings and weekends for data collection if needed, adjusting your time off accordingly Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education: Preferred Qualifications · Prior primary data collection experience, particularly in the area of physical activity · Familiarity with Qualtrics and wearable devices for accessing data · Ability to successfully multitask and work in fast-paced environments · Great communication skills Duties and Responsibilities The NIH -funded R01 SKyRoCKeT Study is an innovative critical step to provide a coherent, interpretable, objectively monitored step-based intensity metric to inform (inter)national physical activity (PA) guidelines by transforming currently vague guidelines of PA intensity into quantifiable PA recommendations that can be of widespread use. The SKyRoCKeT Study will allow for a user-friendly, interpretable metric to more accurately inform public health/behavioral interventions, assessments, analyses, school-based health and physical education curricula and programs, and public health messages for young people and their caregivers. To learn more about the study, please visit: https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT05770817 . The NIH -funded R01 SKyRoCKeT study is seeking an additional postdoctoral research fellow to primarily support data processing, management, checking, and analysis. The postdoc will also contribute to grants management throughout the project lifecycle in areas such as participant recruitment; data collection in the lab as needed; leading and/or collaborating on manuscripts, presentations, and/or reports; and additional responsibilities as needed. Note: This position will not be able to sponsor a visa, including for any possible renewal term. The position is housed within the Department of Public Health & Health Administration, and the postdoctoral researcher will collaborate with team members from the Department of Applied Physiology, Health, & Clinical Sciences. Both departments are housed within the College of Health & Human Services. Specific Responsibilities ● Complete trainings required for the study team, including (but not limited to): CITI Research Trainings (Data or Specimens Only Research, Good Clinical Practice, Social & Behavioral Research/ IRB , Biomedical Research/ IRB , Health Information Privacy & Security); Minors on Campus Training; First Aid, CPR , & AED Training ● Primary responsibilities will be to perform data processing, management, checking, and analysis in Excel and R (primarily), thus familiarity with R is required ● Familiarity with Qualtrics for accessing data is preferred ● Participate in other grant-related activities, which may include (though not limited to): ○ Active recruitment of youth participants at events on and off campus, and throughout the local community ○ Lead and/or collaborate on manuscripts, conference presentations, and/or reports, etc. ○ Liaise, as needed, with representatives/staff in offices across campus such as with post-award, HR, etc. ○ If needed, conduct participant study orientations and obtain participant consent ● Assist in training junior staff to ensure consistency in results during testing and to achieve high-quality data acquisition ● Attend weekly full study team meetings and any additional sub-team meetings specifically regarding issues related to data collection, data management, etc. ● Attend weekly data management meetings among study postdocs and Dr. Gunn to discuss and address data management and statistical analysis ● Contribute to collaborative team discussions regarding issues that may arise throughout the study, study progress, etc. ● Participate in additional projects related to research dissemination, including (but not limited to) literature reviews or validating video recordings ● If needed, perform data collection. This may involve the following: assisting with setting up equipment, ensuring it is calibrated and functioning correctly, preparing the testing environment according to procedural standards, performing anthropometric measurements, administering tests to determine metabolic rates, recording test results, using GoPro camera to record testing protocol, and manually tallying steps ● Maintain accurate records and documentation related to the research study, including downloading wearable device data and forms post-testing, etc. ● Other duties as needed/assigned Other Work/Responsibilities Necessary Licenses or Certifications Proposed Hire Date 12/01/2026 Contact Information Dr. Laura Gunn, Professor & NIH -Funded SKyRoCKeT Study PI laura.gunn@charlotte.edu Expected Length of Assignment 1 year (with the possibility of a short-term renewal) contingent on funding Posting Open Date 10/23/2026 Posting Close Date Special Notes to Applicants Application materials must be submitted through jobs.charlotte.edu and should include: · Resume/CV · Cover letter · Contact information for 2 references · Unofficial Transcripts · Note: This position will not be able to sponsor a visa, including for any possible renewal term. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Unofficial Transcripts Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71456": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Teaching Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Lecturer/Teaching Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Lecturer/Teaching Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications must be made electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu (use position number #002763 ) and must include: (1) a letter of interest that addresses the applicant’s qualifications for the position; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; (4) unofficial copies of undergraduate and graduate transcripts; and (5) contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 23rd, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Jessica White ( jessica.white@charlotte.edu ). Please use the subject line Chemistry Faculty Position in all email inquiries about this position. Required qualifications include a master’s or doctoral degree (or the equivalent) in chemistry or a closely related area, and at least one semester of teaching experience at the college level. Preferred qualifications include at least one year of experience teaching introductory chemistry courses and experience in curriculum/pedagogy development. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree and submit to a criminal background check. Position Information General Information Position Number 002763 Working Title Lecturer/Teaching Professor Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Science (Col) Department Chemistry Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Chemistry Department is dedicated to excellence in education, research, and community engagement. The department is home to 28 full-time faculty members, 9 full-time staff members, more than 200 undergraduate majors, and over 40 graduate students, the department offers B.A., B.S. ( ACS certified) and M.S. degrees in chemistry, and it offers a Ph.D. in Chemistry and Nanoscale Science. The Department is dedicated to the success and professional development of all students; engaging undergraduate and graduate students in collaborative, innovative scientific research; and supporting the local, regional, national, and international communities through leadership and outreach. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Duties include teaching introductory chemistry courses, primarily General Chemistry; supporting departmental curriculum/pedagogy development; and serving on department and university committees. Additional duties could include serving as the coordinator of introductory courses or teaching in other chemistry courses. The successful candidate is also required to provide service to the institution and profession. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Preferred qualifications include at least one year of experience teaching introductory chemistry courses and experience in curriculum/pedagogy development. We especially welcome applicants with experience and interest in utilizing evidence-based scientific teaching and learning practices to foster student success in general education and introductory chemistry courses. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Applications must be made electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu (use position number #002763 ) and must include: (1) a letter of interest that addresses the applicant’s qualifications for the position; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; (4) unofficial copies of undergraduate and graduate transcripts; and (5) contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 23rd, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Jessica White ( jessica.white@charlotte.edu ). Please use the subject line Chemistry Faculty Position in all email inquiries about this position. Required qualifications include a master’s or doctoral degree (or the equivalent) in chemistry or a closely related area, and at least one semester of teaching experience at the college level. Preferred qualifications include at least one year of experience teaching introductory chemistry courses and experience in curriculum/pedagogy development. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree and submit to a criminal background check. Job Open Date 10/23/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/23/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Unofficial Transcripts Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
