    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71413": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. NOTE: Hiring two Assistant Professors from this posting - Positions 4752 and 2598. The candidate chosen for these positions will be subject to a criminal background check and will be required to submit an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 004752, 002598 Working Title Assistant Professor Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College College of Health & Human Svc (Col) Department Applied Physiology, Health, and Clinical Sciences Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Applied Physiology, Health, and Clinical Sciences ( APHCS ; aphcs.charlotte.edu ) is a dynamic unit with an interdisciplinary focus. It is one of four academic units within the College of Health and Human Services. Our programs include: B.S. in Exercise Science ( CAAHEP -accredited) with concentrations in health and fitness, pre-professional, and strength and conditioning ( CASCE -accredited); B.S. in Sports Analytics; M.S. in Kinesiology consisting of an Applied Physiology Concentration (research focus), a CAAHEP -accredited clinical exercise physiology concentration, and a CASCE -accredited strength and conditioning concentration; M.S. in Athletic Training ( CAATE -accredited); B.S. in Respiratory Therapy; and M.S. in Respiratory Care with an Advanced Respiratory Care Practice concentration (CoARC-accredited). Additionally, the Department has faculty affiliated with Ph.D. Programs in Biology, Data Sciences, Epidemiology, and Public Health Sciences. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities Successful candidates will teach courses within the undergraduate Exercise Science and graduate Kinesiology programs. They may also have the opportunity to teach in related APHCS programs (strength & conditioning, nutrition, sports analytics, athletic training), depending on expertise. They will be expected to actively mentor and engage students in research, experiential learning, community engagement, and workforce development while fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. The faculty members will establish a productive program of scholarship in exercise science or a related field, conduct and publish original research, and seek funding to support their independent research agenda. Candidates must possess expertise sufficient to teach exercise physiology and/or related courses. Additional expertise in advanced exercise physiology, clinical exercise physiology, strength & conditioning, nutrition, and/or interprofessional education is highly preferred and will receive special consideration. Additionally, successful candidates will contribute meaningful service to the departmental, college, university, and professional initiatives and participate in activities that enhance the impact and visibility of the department. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience The Department gives primary consideration to candidates holding a doctoral degree in Exercise Physiology, Kinesiology, Exercise Science, Rehabilitation Sciences, or a related discipline. ABD candidates will be considered if they provide evidence of degree completion by August 16, 2027. Candidates must possess expertise to contribute to the Department’s exercise physiology curriculum and maintain a focused program of independent research in exercise science or a related discipline, with strong potential to obtain extramural research support. Necessary Licenses/Certifications None required. ACSM - CEP , CSCS , nutrition- and IPE -related credentials are preferred. Special Notes to Applicants NOTE : Hiring two Assistant Professors from this posting – Positions 4752 and 2598. The candidate chosen for these positions will be subject to a criminal background check and will be required to submit an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/19/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/17/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Dr. Tricia Turner, Search Committee Chair Email: thubbar1@charlotte.edu Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Research Interest Statement Teaching Philosophy Contact Information for References Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71401": {
      "title": "Honeywell Distinguished Scholar or Professor in Infrastructure",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Honeywell Distinguished Scholar or Professor in Infrastructure Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Honeywell Distinguished Scholar or Professor in Infrastructure Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is committed to equality of educational and employment opportunity and does not discriminate against applicants, students, or employees based on any protected status. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check and will be required to submit an official transcript of their highest earned degree. Applications for position #004549 must be submitted electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu and must include a Cover letter that articulates how your qualifications and experience make you a viable candidate for this Distinguished Scholar appointment, Curriculum vitae Statement of research, teaching, and leadership List of three references that can validate academic experience and/or professional expertise. All requested information/documentation must be submitted for applications to be considered. Review of applications will commence on January 1, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled, with an anticipated start date of August 15, 2027. Position Information General Information Position Number 004549 Working Title Honeywell Distinguished Scholar or Professor in Infrastructure Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College College of Engineering (Col) Department Civil and Environmental Engineering Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at UNC Charlotte has approximately 600 undergraduate students and 75 graduate students. The faculty value student-faculty engagement, excellent undergraduate and graduate instruction, and a collaborative and supportive research culture. The Department has exceptional teaching, laboratory, and research facilities. The mild climate, proximity to the Blue Ridge Mountains, access to East Coast beaches, and robust cultural scene make Charlotte, North Carolina a great place to live and work. The Department boasts state-of-the-art laboratory facilities, including the EPIC High Bay Structures Lab that offers a one-million-pound capacity strong wall, multiple geotechnical pits, and extensive experimental capabilities for dynamic loading. The laboratory also has dedicated support personnel for large-scale structural testing. Candidates must exhibit a strong record of scholarly achievement commensurate with appointment at the Associate or full Professor level, including a robust publication history in high-impact journals, successful grant funding, and evidence of research impact, as well as demonstrate excellence in teaching and mentoring at the university level. Candidates entering at the Associate Professor rank should exhibit potential for advancement to the rank of Professor in a near-term horizon, as evidenced by their trajectory in research productivity, leadership, and contributions to the field. Demonstrated evidence of successful collaboration and leadership of multiple investigator proposals and projects is desirable. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The UNC Charlotte Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering ( CEE ) and the School of Energy invite applications for a tenured faculty position in Structural engineering, at the rank of Associate or Full Professor as the Honeywell Distinguished Scholar in Infrastructure or Honeywell Distinguished Professor in Infrastructure. The endowment for this position provides support of the research and scholarship of the appointee. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2027. The successful candidate will join a dynamic, interdisciplinary team committed to advancing innovative civil structures and energy infrastructure. The ideal candidate would have expertise that complements existing strengths of the Structures faculty to facilitate partnerships in new areas of research, utilize key research laboratories, and contribute to the College of Engineering’s established research centers, including the Energy Production and Infrastructure Center ( EPIC ) and the Charlotte Aviation Innovation and Research ( AIR ) Institute. Candidates will be responsible for (1) developing and maintaining a nationally/internationally recognized research program, including securing external funding and publishing high-quality research in peer-reviewed journals; (2) contributing to undergraduate and graduate curricula, including core courses in structural analysis and design, as well as developing new courses aligned with infrastructure systems and emerging trends; (3) mentoring and advising research assistants and postdoctoral scholars; (4) engaging in departmental, university, and professional service, including mentoring junior faculty and serving on technical committees; and (5) fostering interdisciplinary partnerships within the university and externally with stakeholders in government, industry, and non-profits. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Required qualifications include a Ph.D. in civil engineering or a closely related field. Necessary Licenses/Certifications While not required, candidates with a Professional Engineering (PE) license, or willingness to obtain one, are particularly encouraged to apply. Special Notes to Applicants The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is committed to equality of educational and employment opportunity and does not discriminate against applicants, students, or employees based on any protected status. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check and will be required to submit an official transcript of their highest earned degree. Applications for position #004549 must be submitted electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu and must include a Cover letter that articulates how your qualifications and experience make you a viable candidate for this Distinguished Scholar appointment, Curriculum vitae Statement of research, teaching, and leadership List of three references that can validate academic experience and/or professional expertise. All requested information/documentation must be submitted for applications to be considered. Review of applications will commence on January 1, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled, with an anticipated start date of August 15, 2027. Job Open Date 10/21/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 01/01/2027 Proposed Hire Date 08/15/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Statement of Research, Teaching & Leadership Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71391": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The Department of Communication Studies and the Office of Interdisciplinary Studies in the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences (CHESS) at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invite applications for an Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor in Film Studies beginning August 16, 2027. Rank is dependent upon education and experience. Applicants must apply electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu/ (position #5293) and attach: (1) a detailed application letter responding to the job qualifications and description; (2) a one-page statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy; and (3) a current CV. Additional materials may be requested at later stages of the search. For further information, please contact the search committee chair, Prof. Rodney Stringfellow, at rstringf@charlotte.edu. Applications received by November 23, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest earned degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number 005293 Working Title Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies Classification Title 9-Mo Non-Tenure Track Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department Communication Studies Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department Interdisciplinary Studies. The Office of Interdisciplinary Studies promotes interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement within the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences and beyond. The Office of Interdisciplinary Studies is home to a B.A. and M.A. in Interdisciplinary Studies, a B.A. and M.A. in Latin American Studies, graduate certificates in Gerontology and Women and Gender Studies, and multiple interdisciplinary minors: American Studies, Artificial Intelligence Fundamentals, Capitalism Studies, Film Studies, Gerontology, Health & Medical Humanities, Latin American Studies, Legal Studies, and Women’s and Gender Studies. Film Studies is one of the largest and fastest-growing undergraduate programs, with a current enrollment of approximately 250 students across the minor, Video Production certificate, and Film & Media Production concentration in the Interdisciplinary Studies major. For more information, visit https://filmstudies.charlotte.edu/ and https://interdisciplinarystudies.charlotte.edu/ . Communication Studies. The Department of Communication Studies ( https://communication.charlotte.edu/ ) has 53 full-time faculty with areas of emphasis in health communication; organizational communication; public relations; media and technology studies; rhetoric, culture, and social change; and writing, rhetoric and digital studies. The department serves approximately 870 Communication Studies majors and minors and 25 Master’s students. The department’s primary mission is to provide high quality instruction to its undergraduate and graduate students and to contribute significantly to the understanding of communication issues as they affect people’s lives, especially in the Charlotte metropolitan region. Through its teaching, scholarship and service the department is committed to serving diverse populations. Setting. As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Non-Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate teaching goals. Teaching – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, and grading student work. If conducting or supporting research is an element of the work required in this position, then the following statement is applicable: This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. Essential Duties and Responsibilities This non-tenure track faculty member will teach four undergraduate courses per semester or the equivalent on a three-year renewable appointment. The selected faculty member will be expected to teach media production-focused courses in the growing Film Studies curriculum, which includes an undergraduate minor, a Video Production certificate, and the Film & Media Production concentration in the Interdisciplinary Studies major. The selected candidate will be asked to teach a section of the Film program’s capstone course ( FILM 4690) and may also teach electives in their area of specialty and General Education courses. Although the position will primarily involve teaching and service in the Office of Interdisciplinary Studies, the selected candidate will have their appointment home in Communications Studies. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned masters and/or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Candidates must have a master’s or doctoral degree in a field related to Film Studies at the time of appointment and provide evidence of ability to teach a diverse student population at the undergraduate level. Qualifications include prior experience teaching all levels of undergraduate film courses at the university level and a demonstrated commitment to quality teaching. Candidates should be able to demonstrate achievement as a working filmmaker with a history of festival/gallery exhibitions, distribution, grant awards, and/or film industry connections; experience with all phases of film and media production (pre-production, production, post-production); as well as a working knowledge of industry trends, distribution methods, and festival experience. Ideal candidates will also have established industry relationships and contacts within the film and media community. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching and mentoring practices engage students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The Department of Communication Studies and the Office of Interdisciplinary Studies in the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences ( CHESS ) at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte invite applications for an Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor in Film Studies beginning August 16, 2027. Rank is dependent upon education and experience. Applicants must apply electronically at https://jobs.charlotte.edu/ (position #5293) and attach: (1) a detailed application letter responding to the job qualifications and description; (2) a one-page statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy; and (3) a current CV. Additional materials may be requested at later stages of the search. For further information, please contact the search committee chair, Prof. Rodney Stringfellow, at rstringf@charlotte.edu. Applications received by November 23, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest earned degree upon hire. Hiring is subject to budget availability. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Job Open Date 10/21/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/23/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information For further information, please contact the search committee chair, Prof. Rodney Stringfellow, at rstring@charlotte.edu. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/71388": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science and Public Administration",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Assistant Professor of Political Science and Public Administration Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Assistant Professor of Political Science and Public Administration Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the Department of Political Science and Public Administration with a research and teaching focus in emergency management and related focus areas, including disaster governance, risk and security threat assessment and planning, homeland security, community resilience, crisis management, or emergency preparedness and response. The chosen candidate is expected to be able to teach in the MPA core curriculum and offer emergency management electives at the graduate and undergraduate levels. This nine-month, tenure-track appointment begins August 16, 2027. Salary is competitive. It is expected that this is a research-intensive position with a standard 2-2 course load. Candidates must apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu to position #003109. Interested candidates should submit: a cover letter stating their interest in the position, a CV, a writing sample representative of their research, and contact information for three references. Letters of recommendation will only be requested for finalist candidates. Applications received by November 20, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. For more information, please contact the chair of the search committee, Dr. Justin Stritch, at jstritch@charlotte.edu. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. Position Information General Information Position Number 003109 Working Title Assistant Professor of Political Science and Public Administration Classification Title 9-Mo Tenure-Track/ Tenured Faculty College Col of Hum & Erth and Soc Sci (Col) Department Political Science and Public Administration Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 9 Primary Purpose of Department The Department of Political Science and Public Administration has award-winning faculty and nationally ranked programs. The Gerald G. Fox MPA Program offers a NASPAA -accredited Master of Public Administration. U.S. News & World Report ranks the program as 12th in Nonprofit Management, 18th in Local Government Management, 19th in Budget and Finance, 32nd in Public Management, and 47th overall among all Public Affairs Programs. The program offers certificates in Emergency Management, Nonprofit Management, Urban Management and Policy, and Public Budgeting and Finance. The Department also offers a B.A. in Political Science, and minors in Political Science, Public Administration, and Security and Intelligence Studies. The Department is a core contributor to the interdisciplinary Public Policy Ph.D. Program. For additional information about our department, visit our website at https://politicalscience.charlotte.edu/ . More information on the Gerald G. Fox MPA Program can be found here: https://mpa.charlotte.edu/ . As the largest and most academically diverse college at UNC Charlotte, the College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences houses 14 departments, 13 interdisciplinary programs, and 7 applied centers. We offer a wide range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs with extensive honors and early-entry options. The College plays a pivotal role in delivering on the university’s goals in the areas of research excellence, student success and experience, and community engagement. Through their scholarship and teaching, the College’s 350+ faculty members seek to deepen our understanding of complex problems and lead the university in interdisciplinary collaboration. UNC Charlotte is an R-1 University, and the only research university in North Carolina’s largest metropolitan area. As the second-largest institution in the UNC System, we transform lives, communities, and industries through accessible and affordable education, offering exemplary bachelor’s, master’s, doctoral, and professional programs. Our commitment to scholarship, innovation, and service drives our contributions to the ever-evolving needs of society. Through strategic partnerships and a focus on real-world applications, we prepare our graduates to excel in their careers and lead with purpose and integrity in a rapidly changing world. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work The University of North Carolina at Charlotte recognizes the differentiation of mission, goals, and objectives inherent in the diversity of disciplines represented by its colleges and departments. Thus, the Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure Track Faculty should be interpreted in the context of the related departmental and collegiate goals. Teaching, Advising, Curriculum and Instructional Development – Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to teaching may include but are not limited to: subject matter competence, course design, course presentation, advising and consultation, directing student research, curriculum and instructional development, and grading student work. Scholarly Research, Creative, and Other Professional Activities – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to participate in expanding the knowledge base of their academic field by conducting research or engaging in other creative activities as appropriate to the faculty member’s discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to research may include but are not limited to: research/creative activity that generates new knowledge and/or synthesizes and/or integrates existing knowledge, publication, an on-going agenda for research, scholarly, and creative activity, and are expected to seek extramural support for their work. Service to University, the Public, and the Profession – All tenured and tenure-track faculty are expected to engage in a program of service appropriate to their discipline. Faculty responsibilities and essential functions with respect to service may include but are not limited to: service to the administration and governance of the University, public service, and service to the profession. This position is subject to research-security screening. As part of the application, applicants must disclose, either in their CV or in a separate document submitted with the application , all current appointments or affiliations with foreign institutions or organizations, foreign research support, and ongoing research collaborations with foreign institutions, organizations, or their personnel. Before candidates advance to an on-campus interview, the university will review this information and other publicly available information for relationships involving institutions on the current Section 1286 list and any other applicable U.S. restricted-party lists. A disclosed or identified relationship does not automatically disqualify a candidate. As a condition of employment, a selected candidate must resolve any relationship the university determines would violate applicable law or sponsor requirements or materially impair the candidate’s or the university’s eligibility for federal funding. To view the complete Job Responsibilities and Essential Functions for Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty document visit our website: https://facultyhandbooks.charlotte.edu/full-time-faculty-handbook/ Essential Duties and Responsibilities The chosen candidate is expected to be able to teach in the MPA core curriculum and offer emergency management electives at the graduate and undergraduate levels. It is expected that this is a research-intensive position with a standard 2-2 course load. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Required Qualifications: Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Public Administration, Political Science, Public Policy, or a related social science discipline with a substantive focus on topics in emergency management by the start date of the position. Candidates must have an active research agenda consistent with expectations of an R1 institution and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants The University of North Carolina at Charlotte invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the Department of Political Science and Public Administration with a research and teaching focus in emergency management and related focus areas, including disaster governance, risk and security threat assessment and planning, homeland security, community resilience, crisis management, or emergency preparedness and response. The chosen candidate is expected to be able to teach in the MPA core curriculum and offer emergency management electives at the graduate and undergraduate levels. This nine-month, tenure-track appointment begins August 16, 2027. Salary is competitive. It is expected that this is a research-intensive position with a standard 2-2 course load. Candidates must apply online at https://jobs.charlotte.edu to position #003109. Interested candidates should submit: a cover letter stating their interest in the position, a CV, a writing sample representative of their research, and contact information for three references. Letters of recommendation will only be requested for finalist candidates. Applications received by November 20, 2026 are assured full consideration although recruitment will continue until the position is filled. For more information, please contact the chair of the search committee, Dr. Justin Stritch, at jstritch@charlotte.edu. Finalists will be asked to discuss how their teaching practices engage, include, and eliminate barriers to learning for UNC Charlotte students, and how their educational approach helps increase the engagement and success of all students regardless of their entry pathway into the university. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to Criminal Background Check. The candidate selected for this position will be required to submit their official transcripts of their highest degree upon hire. Job Open Date 10/21/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 11/20/2026 Proposed Hire Date 08/16/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information For more information, please contact the chair of the search committee, Dr. Justin Stritch, at jstritch@charlotte.edu. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Optional Documents Other Document Other Document 2 Other Document 3 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
